  "description": "How to test your code in a coding interview: dry runs with a variable table, edge cases by data type, finding off-by-one errors, numeric traps, fixing bugs calmly, and stress testing at home.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"3\">Level 3 - Intermediate</span>\n    <span class=\"time\">45 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip accent\">Interview Hub</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">SDE-1 / SDE-2 / SDE-3</span>\n  </div>\n  <h1>Testing your code in the interview</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Writing the code is half the round. The other half is showing, without being asked, that it works: a dry run with a variable table, a quick sweep of edge cases chosen by data type, a hunt for off-by-one errors, and a calm, small fix when something breaks. This page gives you a fixed routine for all four, plus a stress-test template you can run at home.</p>\n  <p class=\"small prereq\"><b>Before this page:</b> <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-start-here\">How coding rounds work</a> and <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-complexity\">Complexity and Big-O</a>. <b>Pairs well with:</b> <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-binary-search\">Binary search</a> (the home of off-by-one bugs) and <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-recent-questions\">Recently asked questions</a>.</p>\n"
  },
